[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Generics, Type Erasure, and Variance in Object-Oriented Programming"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generics, type erasure, and variance are advanced type-system concepts that allow developers to write flexible, reusable, and type-safe code in object-oriented programming languages like Java, C#, and Kotlin."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Generics"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generics enable types (classes and interfaces) to be parameters when defining classes, interfaces, and methods. This lets you write a single class or method that can work with different data types while providing compile-time type safety."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public class Box<T> {
    private T item;

    public void setItem(T item) {
        this.item = item;
    }

    public T getItem() {
        return item;
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Type Erasure"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Type erasure is a mechanism used by compilers (such as in Java) to implement generics. The compiler examines the generic code to ensure type safety and then removes all generic type parameters, replacing them with their bounds (or Object if unbounded) in the generated bytecode. This ensures backward compatibility with older versions of the runtime that do not support generics natively."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Generic type information is not available at runtime (e.g., you cannot check if a List<T> is a List<String> via instanceof)."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Type casts are inserted automatically by the compiler behind the scenes."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Variance: Covariance and Contravariance"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Variance refers to how subtyping between more complex types relates to subtyping between their component types. If Cat is a subtype of Animal, how does List<Cat> relate to List<Animal>?"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Invariant: List<Cat> is neither a supertype nor a subtype of List<Animal>. Generics are invariant by default in languages like Java."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Covariance: Preserves the ordering of types (subtype remains a subtype). Denoted in Java using wildcards with extends, like List<? extends Animal>."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Contravariance: Reverses the ordering of types (accepts supertypes). Denoted in Java using wildcards with super, like List<? super Cat>."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Practical Application: PECS Principle"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"A famous guideline for handling variance in Java is the PECS mnemonic: Producer Extends, Consumer Super. Use '? extends T' when your data structure only produces data (covariant), and use '? super T' when your data structure only consumes data (contravariant)."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"public void copyData(List<? extends Animal> source, List<? super Animal> destination) {
    for (Animal animal : source) {
        destination.add(animal);
    }
}"}]
